# {{project_name}}

Role-based access control — grant, revoke and check roles, with an admin role
that administers the other roles. Generated by
[soroban-forge](https://github.com/soroban-forge-labs/soroban-forge) from the
`access-control` template.

Use it instead of scattering `caller == admin` checks through a contract: roles
are named, several addresses can hold one, and each role can be delegated to a
different administrator without touching the entrypoints it guards.

## Features

- `grant_role` / `revoke_role` / `has_role` — the primitives, gated on each
  role's admin role
- `get_role_admin` / `set_role_admin` — `ADMIN_ROLE` administers every role by
  default; hand any role to a different administrator with `set_role_admin`
- `renounce_role` — give up one of your own roles; cannot touch anyone else's
- `role_member_count` — how many addresses hold a role
- `mint` / `burn` — two demo entrypoints, gated on `MINTER_ROLE` and
  `BURNER_ROLE`, over a small internal ledger

## Develop

```sh
cargo test
stellar contract build
```

## Deploy to testnet

The constructor takes `admin`, the address that receives `ADMIN_ROLE`:

```sh
stellar contract deploy \
  --wasm target/wasm32v1-none/release/{{crate_name}}.wasm \
  --source-account <your-identity> \
  --network testnet \
  -- --admin <ADMIN_ADDRESS>
```

Grant a role:

```sh
stellar contract invoke \
  --id <CONTRACT_ID> \
  --source-account <ADMIN_IDENTITY> \
  --network testnet \
  -- \
  grant_role \
  --caller <ADMIN_ADDRESS> \
  --role minter \
  --account <MINTER_ADDRESS>
```

## Gating your own entrypoint on a role

Three steps.

**1. Name the role.** Roles are `Symbol`s, so declare a const rather than
writing the string at each call site — a typo does not fail, it silently
creates a *different* role that nobody holds:

```rust
pub const PAUSER_ROLE: Symbol = symbol_short!("pauser");
```

`symbol_short!` is limited to 9 characters. Nothing needs to register the role
first; a role exists as soon as someone is granted it.

**2. Take a `caller` and guard on it.** `require_role` is the whole gate — it
checks that `caller` authorized this invocation *and* holds the role, and
panics with `Error::Unauthorized` otherwise:

```rust
pub fn pause(env: Env, caller: Address) {
    require_role(&env, &caller, &PAUSER_ROLE);
    // ... the protected work
}
```

The caller is an explicit argument because any number of addresses may hold a
role, so the contract cannot infer who is acting. Put `require_role` first, and
do not call `caller.require_auth()` yourself — `require_role` already does.

**3. Decide who administers it.** By default `ADMIN_ROLE` grants and revokes
every role. To delegate, point the role at another one:

```rust
// holders of MANAGER_ROLE may now grant and revoke PAUSER_ROLE
client.set_role_admin(&admin, &PAUSER_ROLE, &MANAGER_ROLE);
```

`set_role_admin` itself always requires `ADMIN_ROLE`, so a delegated
administrator cannot re-parent the role graph.

To gate an entrypoint on *either* of two roles, check them explicitly instead:

```rust
pub fn pause(env: Env, caller: Address) {
    caller.require_auth();
    if !Self::has_role(env.clone(), PAUSER_ROLE, caller.clone())
        && !Self::has_role(env.clone(), ADMIN_ROLE, caller)
    {
        panic_with_error!(&env, Error::Unauthorized);
    }
    // ...
}
```

## Behaviour worth knowing

- **Grant and revoke are idempotent.** Granting a role an account already holds
  changes nothing and emits no event; so does revoking one it does not hold.
  That keeps `role_member_count` honest and means two administrators acting
  concurrently cannot make one of them fail.
- **The last admin cannot be removed.** `revoke_role` and `renounce_role` reject
  the final `ADMIN_ROLE` holder with `Error::LastAdmin`. Without it, one
  transaction can leave a contract where no role can ever be granted again.
  Delete that check in `revoke()` if you deliberately want a contract that can
  be made permanently admin-less.
- **Roles live in persistent storage** and every read that finds an entry
  extends its TTL, because an expired entry would read back as "does not hold
  the role" — a rarely-used admin would silently lose its powers.
- **Every state change emits an event** — `RoleGranted`, `RoleRevoked`,
  `RoleAdminChanged` — so an indexer can follow who holds what without
  replaying storage. The role is a topic, so a consumer can subscribe to one
  role rather than filtering every change.
- **Holding `ADMIN_ROLE` does not grant the roles it administers.** An admin
  can make itself a minter, but is not one by default.

## Replacing the demo ledger

`mint`, `burn`, `balance` and `total_supply` exist only to give
`MINTER_ROLE` and `BURNER_ROLE` something real to protect. Delete them, along
with `DataKey::Balance` / `DataKey::TotalSupply`, `Error::InvalidAmount`,
`Error::InsufficientBalance`, the `Mint` / `Burn` events and the read/write
balance helpers. Everything above them is the reusable part.

